  "hints": [
    "The three arrays are read in parallel, so the number of contracts is the length of the shortest of them, and each contract spans the interval between its two moments in whichever order they arrive, so normalise every pair with `min` and `max` first.",
    "A span may follow another only when it begins no earlier than the first one finishes. Sort the contracts by finish and any valid selection then walks forward through that order, so the contract chosen before the current one always comes earlier.",
    "Define `dp[i]` as the best pay using only the first `i` contracts in finish order. For contract `i` you either skip it and keep `dp[i-1]`, or take it and add `profit[i]` to the best schedule that finishes no later than its begin. That predecessor is the last contract with `finish <= begin`, and because the array is sorted by finish it is a lower-bound query: binary search it and use `dp[j] + profit[i]`. `dp[0] = 0` covers taking nothing."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Both versions start the same way: read the arrays over the shortest of the three lengths, turn each contract into the span between its two moments (`begin` is the smaller of the pair, `finish` the larger), and sort those spans by finish, breaking ties by begin and then by original index. On that ordering the classic 1D DP applies, where `dp[i]` is the best pay obtainable from the first `i` contracts: `dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[j] + profit[i])`, and `j` counts the contracts that finish no later than `begin`. Those are exactly the contracts that may precede contract `i`, and their DP values are already final because they finish earlier, so the recurrence is well-founded. The brute force finds `j` by walking backwards one contract at a time, giving O(n\u00b2) overall. The optimal binary searches the sorted array of finish moments for the last index whose finish is `<= begin`, giving O(n log n) overall.",
    "why_optimal": "Once the contracts are normalised and sorted by finish, the only work left is the predecessor lookup, and the finish moments are monotone by construction, so a binary search answers it in about log n comparisons with two integer variables. The sort itself costs O(n log n) and is unavoidable for a comparison-based method, so O(n log n) is the best attainable; at n <= 200 both versions are instant, but the O(n\u00b2) walk is precisely what fails when the same question arrives with 100000 contracts.",
    "pitfalls": "Read the arrays in parallel over the shortest of the three lengths. The three arrays are independent inputs, so a length taken from `startTime` alone walks off the end of a shorter `endTime` or `profit`, and that failure is silent rather than loud: JavaScript yields `undefined` and continues with comparisons that are all false, while Python raises, so the two implementations disagree. Normalise every pair before sorting it. The DP is only exact for spans that actually run forwards: when `startTime[i] > endTime[i]` the pair describes the same stretch read from the other end, and comparing the raw array entries lets the recurrence accept sets that overlap; taking `min` and `max` of the pair first removes that case and makes the recurrence equal to the true maximum for every input the constraints allow. Compatibility is `finish <= begin` and not `<`: the example above only reaches 120 because a contract ending at 3 may be followed by one starting at 3, so a strict comparison silently drops the optimal schedule. Sorting by start instead of finish destroys the DP order. The binary search must return the last index with `finish <= begin`, so its midpoint must round up (`(lo + hi + 1) / 2`); rounding down returns too small a schedule and produces a wrong, smaller answer. `dp[0] = 0` must exist because taking nothing is legal, and the array needs `n + 1` slots so that `dp[i-1]` is safe at `i = 1`."
  },
